@startuml
!include theme.iuml
title Grammar analysis and predictive parsing — snapshot DTOs
hide empty members
class SnapshotModel <<reference>>
class GrammarAnalysis {
  original; transformed: Grammar
  start_symbol: str
  terminals; nonterminals: list[str]
  first; follow: dict[str, list[str]]
  table: dict[str, dict[str, list[str]]]
  is_ll1: bool
  warnings: list[str]
  consistent_symbols(): Self
}
class TransformationStep {
  operation: str
  before; after: Grammar
  description: str
}
class GrammarConflict {
  nonterminal: str
  terminal: str
  productions: list[list[str]]
}
class ParseResult {
  accepted: bool
  error: str | None
  consumed: int {min=0}
  consistent_outcome(): Self
}
class ParseStep {
  stack: list[str]
  remaining: list[str]
  action: str
}
GrammarAnalysis --|> SnapshotModel
TransformationStep --|> SnapshotModel
GrammarConflict --|> SnapshotModel
ParseResult --|> SnapshotModel
ParseStep --|> SnapshotModel
GrammarAnalysis "0..*" --> "0..*" TransformationStep : steps
GrammarAnalysis "0..*" --> "0..*" GrammarConflict : conflicts
ParseResult "0..*" --> "1..*" ParseStep : trace
note bottom of GrammarAnalysis
  Grammar = dict[str, list[list[str]]], not a runtime class.
  Epsilon is an empty RHS; FIRST displays epsilon;
  FOLLOW/table lookahead uses $ for end of input.
  Conflicting table cells are retained in conflicts, not
  resolved by arbitrarily choosing a production.
end note
note bottom of ParseResult
  accepted iff error is None.
  RecordedTest additionally checks consumed token count
  and forbids accepted parsing with a non-LL(1) grammar.
end note
legend bottom
  Source: backend/analyzer_models.py. These are real DTO classes, not the compiler's internal dictionaries.
  Compiler functions create dictionaries; snapshot validation constructs these typed nested values.
  GrammarAnalysis and ParseResult are separately referenced by RecordedTest, not associated with each other.
endlegend
@enduml
